rough the use of journey mapping we  understand a lot more about  our users our consumers the different  personas  shop differently and we're able to  understand their shopping habits  then we are able to better reach them  for their needs  so for example a young bachelor would  probably do a lot more  research online and then they like to go  to their stores  and feel the furniture and ask about  lifestyle tips after that every  marketing effort is a lot more targeted  strategic and obviously more productive  one exercise that we did a couple of  years back  was really looking at how our staff was  interacting with our customers  we actually built cardboard boxes of the  whole counter  and how the customer journey would be  when they move from one end to the other  end what were the pain points during the  whole process  so this actually shortened the queue  time for the customer  from the business point of view we  actually could serve more customers  in the same period of time compared to  previously  are you inspired by what scantique and  soup spoon have achieved  check out the design singapore council's  design for business  user journey mapping scheme a co-funding  assistance program with enterprise  singapore for  smes here are three different  customer journey map options to consider  option one  focuses on the research of one persona  this option is generally great for young  businesses  with a relatively small group of  customers  option two focuses on three to four  personas  it's ideal for companies who want to  expand and explore new customer segments  this option is great for businesses with  different products and deferring  customer needs  and finally option 3 which is a more  intricate system  that uses a service design blueprint  it's a diagram that visualizes the  relationship  among touch points processes and people  it's essentially how back-end operations  affect  front-end interactions with your  customers this option is great for  businesses